<!-- BEGIN baseline:powershell-conventions v0.5.0 -->
## Portable Agent Baseline: PowerShell Conventions

- Wrap any PowerShell function call that can return zero, one, or many items in `@(...)` at every call site (`$x = @(Get-Something ...)`). Never rely on a function's own `return $collection` statement to guarantee array-typed output across the call boundary — PowerShell silently unwraps a single-element array to a scalar, string or object alike. `pwsh` 7+'s synthetic scalar `.Count`/`.Length` convenience can mask exactly this bug, so never treat "works under `pwsh`" as evidence a script works under Windows PowerShell 5.1 for any code path with scalar-vs-array ambiguity; test 0/1/many explicitly on every version the script must support.
- Never embed a literal `\` inside a single string argument standing in for multiple path segments (e.g. `Join-Path $root "baselines\$Pack"`) in any script meant to run under `pwsh` on more than one OS. macOS/Linux `pwsh` treats an embedded backslash as a literal character, not a separator, silently producing a malformed path component. Use forward slashes (`/`) or pass each segment as its own `Join-Path` argument instead.
- Quote a literal `@`-prefixed string argument passed to a PowerShell command — unquoted, it's parsed as splatting syntax and the argument is silently dropped rather than passed through.
- On Windows via git-bash/MSYS, `ln -s` to create a new symlink can silently no-op (no error thrown, and a sandbox override does not help) — use PowerShell's `New-Item -ItemType SymbolicLink` instead, and confirm success with `Get-Item <path> | Select LinkType` rather than trusting the no-error exit code.
- `switch` in PowerShell falls through by default — it is not C#. Every matching branch runs unless each ends in `break`, so with `-Wildcard` and overlapping patterns one input executes several branches and the last assignment wins (`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` matches both `"~/*"` and `"*.md"`). For overlapping patterns where exactly one should win, use `if`/`elseif` or put `break` in every branch, and order the patterns most-specific-first either way. Fall-through is occasionally what you want; the trap is using `switch` as a first-match-wins expression.
- Route a command name that other tools or scripts will also invoke through a PATH-based shim (a real executable on `PATH`), not a shell alias. Shell aliases only expand in an interactive shell session — a non-interactive or scripted invocation (another script, a tool that shells out, a CI job) calls the command name directly, never sees the alias, and silently falls through to the original command instead of the intended override.
- PowerShell variable names are case-insensitive: `$P` and `$p` are the same variable, so a `foreach ($p in ...)` loop silently overwrites a `$P` holding a path, and the failure surfaces as a `Cannot find path` naming the loop's current value rather than as a destroyed variable. Name loop variables for what they iterate (`$pack`, `$repo`, `$file`) and never distinguish two live variables by case alone.
- Apply this baseline before ordinary PowerShell scripting habits, but never use it to override explicit user instructions, safety rules, privacy boundaries, or stricter repo-local instructions.
<!-- END baseline:powershell-conventions -->
